WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.880
For the Ancient Egyptians, the cycle of life&nbsp;
itself began and ended with Isis and Osiris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.400
Their story is one of the oldest known myths, and&nbsp;
it informed spiritual beliefs, power structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.640
and gender roles in Ancient Egypt and beyond. And&nbsp;
who wouldn’t be drawn to a tale that has it all?

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.440
And by all, I mean devious party tricks,&nbsp;
sibling rivalry, shape-shifting  quests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:30.320
true love – and a missing penis.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.400
In 1922, the unsealing of King Tutankhamun’s&nbsp;
tomb opened a portal to Ancient Egypt. 

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.800
In&nbsp;&nbsp;the burial chamber, carvings on the wall show&nbsp;
the young Pharaoh traveling to the afterlife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.760
Here he is welcomed by Isis, mother&nbsp;
of Pharaohs and goddess of protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.640
and Osiris, king of the underworld.
Two of Egypt’s most powerful Gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.080
Isis and Osiris appear throughout funerary&nbsp;
texts and art. King Tut’s iconic death mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.480
bears Osiris’ likeness, while symbols&nbsp;
of Iksis are etched across his coffin.&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.840
The influence of these two deities&nbsp;
extends far beyond mummies and tombs.&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.880
Long before Isis and Osiris,&nbsp;
there was the creator God Atum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.520
Some accounts identify him as the&nbsp;
creator of Shu, god of dry air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.040
and Tefnut, goddess of moisture, who were the&nbsp;
parents of Geb, the earth, and Nut, the sky.&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.720
Other versions say that Atum fashioned Geb&nbsp;
and Nut himself, separating earth from sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.360
when he discovered their illicit affair.&nbsp;
Either way, it’s a formidable family tree.&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.200
Geb and Nut had four children:&nbsp;
Isis, Osiris, Set, and Nephthys.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.520
These deities paired off in the womb, with&nbsp;
Isis and Osiris falling for each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.600
and Set and Nephthys forming their own bond.
As first born, Osiris became ruler of the earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.920
He was essentially the creator of society,&nbsp;
who brought law, farming, religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.680
and education to the people. Not to be outdone,&nbsp;
Isis brought medicine, healing…and beer brewing.&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.600
If Osiris stood for order, Set stood for&nbsp;
chaos. Even with the restrained Nephthys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.440
by his side, he couldn’t contain&nbsp;
his desire to destroy his brother.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.920
Set was known for causing storms,&nbsp;
war, and general malaise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.800
But his most vicious plan began with a party.
At this gathering, Set produced an ornate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.080
chest and proposed a game: whoever could fit&nbsp;
inside the chest, could keep it. All the Gods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.240
tried to squeeze in, but none succeeded – for Set&nbsp;
had designed the chest for Osiris’ body alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.040
As his brother settled in, Set slammed the&nbsp;
chest shut, sealed it, and flung it in the Nile.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:39.947
As Set seized power over Egypt, the chest floated&nbsp;
down the Nile and washed up at the kingdom of Byblos. Here it merged with the trunk&nbsp;of a cedar tree, 

00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:46.480
which the King promptly cut down and used to prop up his palace - not&nbsp;knowing that Osiris’ body was inside.&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.800
The image of this pillar containing the&nbsp;
imprisoned God became the djed , a symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.840
of stability in Egyptian architecture.
Back with the Gods, things were anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.440
but stable. Furious, Isis vowed to find her&nbsp;
husband and put an end to Set’s rule. She and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.160
Nephthys took flight as falcons, questioning&nbsp;
everyone they could find for news of Osiris.&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.440
Eventually, a group of children&nbsp;
pointed them in the right direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.520
Landing on the shores of Byblos, Isis transformed&nbsp;
into a human and set about charming the palace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.520
maids--eventually gaining the favor of the queen.
Now an undercover nanny, Isis took to bathing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.560
infant prince in fire in order to instill some&nbsp;
godly strength – but when the king discovered her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.000
actions he was so terrified that he promised&nbsp;
to give her anything in exchange for safety.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.320
Of course, Isis demanded the chest.
She brought Osiris’ body back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.480
banks of the Nile, hiding it in the reeds&nbsp;
until she could figure out how to revive him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.240
But Set found the body, hacking it into fourteen&nbsp;
pieces and scattering them across Egypt.&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.160
Once again, Isis set out in search of Osiris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.560
She soon tracked down thirteen parts of the&nbsp;
body, which she lovingly stitched back together.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.160
But they had a little problem: Osiris’&nbsp;
penis had been eaten by a fish.&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.840
Isis had come too far to be thwarted by this. Some&nbsp;
say that she fashioned a new member for Osiris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.080
with others adding that she drew out his&nbsp;
last sexual powers with her beating wings.&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:17.040
Either way, the two used Osiris’ final moments on&nbsp;
earth to conceive an heir. By the power of Isis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.360
Osiris was briefly revived, given a proper burial,&nbsp;
and sent safely to the realm of the dead.&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.840
Here he&nbsp;was resurrected as the king of the afterlife,&nbsp;
or Duat, where he would rule for eternity.&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.560
In the wake of Osiris’ departure, Isis raised&nbsp;
their son Horus in hiding. Under her guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.400
he grew into a strapping young God who defeated&nbsp;
Set to assume his rightful rule over Egypt.&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.360
The epic battle between Horus and Set was&nbsp;
sometimes used as a metaphor for the war between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.400
Upper and Lower Egypt, with Horus’ triumph&nbsp;
standing for the unification of the region.&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.040
The story served as a guide to death for all&nbsp;
Ancient Egyptians. Isis’ retrieval of the chest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.560
from Byblos informed the belief that Egyptians&nbsp;
should only be laid to rest on Egyptian soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.880
and the second part of her quest highlights the&nbsp;
importance of protecting the body after death.&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.520
Isis painstakingly searched for&nbsp;
and reassembled Osiris’ body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.320
thus constructing what can be&nbsp;
considered the first mummy.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.074
Her re-creation of the penis corresponds to&nbsp;
the belief that the body should be buried&nbsp;&nbsp;intact.

00:05:20.074 --> 00:05:24.240
During mummification, embalmers&nbsp;
would construct absent body parts for the dead.&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.320
This ensured that they would travel to&nbsp;
the underworld in the best possible shape.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.960
For King Tut, this meant being buried&nbsp;
with his penis propped at a 90-degree angle,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.840
a feature which scholars have&nbsp;
linked to Osiris’ postmortem fertility.&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.680
Embalmers often placed the&nbsp;
dead in decorated coffins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.160
which eventually became kanthropoid or&nbsp;
human-shaped to resemble Osiris’ chest.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.320
Many bore the face of the God, and were&nbsp;
decorated with the wings of Isis and Nephthys.&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.560
The Pyramid texts of the Old Kingdom frame Osiris&nbsp;
as king and judge of the afterlife These writings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.240
were a set of utterances and hymns, intended for&nbsp;
royalty to ensure safe passage to the afterlife.&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.760
To reach paradise, or the field of reeds, you&nbsp;
had to meet with Osiris in the Hall of Truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.600
Here he weighed your heart against&nbsp;
the Feather of Truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.160
which you’d hopefully kept nice and light by&nbsp;
steering clear of bad thoughts and actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.960
If your heart wasn’t weighed&nbsp;
down, Osiris let you pass.&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.920
This encounter between god and mortals was&nbsp;
extended in the Coffin Texts of the Middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.720
Kingdom, which emphasize Osiris as the&nbsp;
judge of all people regardless of class.&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.760
In the New Kingdom, which occurred between&nbsp;
the sixteenth and eleventh century BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.120
the Book of the Dead became the&nbsp;
most popular guide to the afterlife.&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.920
A collection of funerary spells and&nbsp;
guidelines written over hundreds of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.080
the Book guided the dead through face-offs&nbsp;
with demons, confessions to Gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.040
the weighing of the heart, and other judgments&nbsp;
of the spirit. Isis and Osiris appear throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.720
these texts, judging or guiding the&nbsp;
dead through their spiritual reckonings.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.400
While Osiris reveals the challenges and promises&nbsp;
of the afterlife, Isis stands for life itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.440
From food and water to the nourishing&nbsp;
powers of medicine and maternal instincts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.160
Isis presided over living things.
She was widely worshiped as the Protectress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.640
and Mother of All Pharaohs, and is referred&nbsp;
to as “The Great One” in the Pyramid texts.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.200
Her behavior following the death&nbsp;
of Osiris epitomizes the ingenuity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.360
and perseverance that she was beloved for.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.960
This triumph indicates her&nbsp;
possession of mighty magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.880
which allows her to shapeshift, revive&nbsp;
the dead, and protect the vulnerable.&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.320
The awe-inspiring powers of Isis can be linked&nbsp;
to the elevated role of women in Ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:40.480
Egypt. Women had autonomy over&nbsp;
marriage, property, and finances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.000
and could be Pharaohs in their own right.
Non-royal women also served as scribes, doctors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.880
and religious officials, carrying out different&nbsp;
forms of protection that Isis also embodied.&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.440
Over time, Isis became the supreme Egyptian God.&nbsp;
Her temples were erected across North Africa, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:03.200
well as Crete, Pompeii, and Northern Europe. The&nbsp;
Roman cult of Isis is still shrouded in mystery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.480
but we do know that her followers&nbsp;
launched a model ship filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.720
offerings to the Goddess every year.
In the classical world, she was more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.280
generally associated with the Goddess of&nbsp;
food and fertility Demeter, and her cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.480
in Rome came to rival that of Christianity.
It’s also been noted that Christian iconography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.960
of the Madonna and child borrows from the&nbsp;
image of Isis protecting the infant Horus.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.240
From mummification to mothering, she&nbsp;
informed a host of practices and ideals.&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.920
From temple paintings to burial&nbsp;
practices to secret rituals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:37.600
Osiris and Isis infused ancient&nbsp;
iconography and beliefs.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.320
Our knowledge of their story myth comes&nbsp;
from a similarly diverse range of sources.&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.520
Found on the walls of ancient pyramids - and&nbsp;
aptly named the Pyramid Texts - the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.560
religious writings in the world reference the&nbsp;
grisly murder of Osiris. Later texts like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.760
Chester Beatty Papyrus and the Shabaka Stone&nbsp;also allude to this event and its fallout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.880
while classical historians like Plutarch and Diodorus&nbsp;Siculus wrote their own versions of the myth.&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.000
While the premature death of Osiris and his&nbsp;
separation from Isis could be seen as a tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.960
tale, the story reveals the Ancient Egyptian&nbsp;
belief that the boundaries between material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.320
and spiritual, life and death, are porous.
For the Ancient Egyptians, Osiris offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.240
a poignant reminder that life&nbsp;
was fragile – even for a God.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.080
As the great protector, Isis offered&nbsp;
guidance on that fragile path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.002
and ensured that the channels between&nbsp;
the living and the lost were always open.

